{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-186", "title": "Typical case timelines", "last_reviewed": "2026-05-05", "audience": "clients", "body": "Many claims resolve within nine to eighteen months, but every case is different. Cases usually cannot be valued until the client has finished treatment or reached maximum medical improvement.

Once treatment ends, the firm gathers records and bills, which commonly takes six to ten weeks. A demand package is then sent to the insurer, and insurers often take thirty to sixty days to respond.

If a fair offer is not made, the attorney may recommend filing a lawsuit, which can add a year or more. Only the attorney can give a case-specific estimate of how long a matter will take.", "related": ["Understanding our fee agreement", "Language access"]}]}

{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-137", "title": "Case costs and expenses", "last_reviewed": "2026-09-09", "audience": "clients", "body": "Case costs are separate from attorney fees and include filing fees, deposition transcripts, medical record charges, and expert fees. The firm advances case costs during the matter.

