{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-186", "title": "Typical case timelines", "last_reviewed": "2026-05-05", "audience": "clients", "body": "Many claims resolve within nine to eighteen months, but every case is different. Cases usually cannot be valued until the client has finished treatment or reached maximum medical improvement.

Once treatment ends, the firm gathers records and bills, which commonly takes six to ten weeks. A demand package is then sent to the insurer, and insurers often take thirty to sixty days to respond.

If a fair offer is not made, the attorney may recommend filing a lawsuit, which can add a year or more. Only the attorney can give a case-specific estimate of how long a matter will take.", "related": ["Understanding our fee agreement", "Language access"]}]}

{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-466", "title": "Preparing for a meeting with your attorney", "last_reviewed": "2026-02-28", "audience": "clients", "body": "Case review meetings last about forty-five minutes, and there is no charge for them. Clients should bring photo identification, insurance cards, and any documents or photos about the incident.

The attorney will review the facts, explain options, and answer questions about the process. Clients may bring a family member, though the attorney may ask to speak privately for part of the meeting.

Video consultations are available for clients who cannot travel.", "related": ["Confidentiality and privacy", "Case updates and communication"]}]}

{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-212", "title": "Documents to gather", "last_reviewed": "2026-03-09", "audience": "clients", "body": "Helpful documents include medical bills, visit summaries, photos, the police or incident report, and insurance cards. For lost wages, recent pay stubs and a letter from the employer confirming missed time are useful.

Clients should keep a simple log of symptoms, appointments, and out-of-pocket expenses. Original documents should be kept by the client; copies or phone photos are fine for the firm.

Documents can be uploaded through the client portal or emailed to the assigned paralegal. Clients should not alter or annotate original records before sending them.", "related": ["Medical bills during an open claim", "Language access"]}]}

{"results": [{"article_id": "KB-137", "title": "Case costs and expenses", "last_reviewed": "2026-09-09", "audience": "clients", "body": "Case costs are separate from attorney fees and include filing fees, deposition transcripts, medical record charges, and expert fees. The firm advances case costs during the matter.

